WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:04.740
A handful of kid-vid writers have taken it
upon themselves to close out their series

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.820
with some spectacularly definitive — and
dark — final episodes.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.400
Here's a look at some of the darkest series
finales of kids' TV shows.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.840
Superman: The Animated Series

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.780
The finale of Superman: The Animated Series
finds Superman going full evil, but it's not

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.540
his fault, as Darkseid has brainwashed him
into unleashing death and destruction.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:29.880
After Superman attacks some military bases,
Supergirl tries to stop him and nearly gets

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:34.230
killed in the process, which is when Lois
Lane shows up and breaks the trance.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:38.100
Later on, Superman awakens in a military prison,
awaiting execution.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:43.120
But he manages to escape and make his way
back to Darkseid's palace for a final, extremely

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.220
violent confrontation.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.660
Superman nearly kills the bad guy, but instead
he implores Darkseid's oppressed followers

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.020
to do with him what they wish.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.240
Forever devoted, they whisk his body away
for medical treatment.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.320
As for Superman, he has to deal with a planet
full of people who feel betrayed by their

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.180
one-time protector, unaware of the brainwashing.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:06.700
"Onward, for the glory of Darkseid and Apokolips."

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.560
The World of David the Gnome

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:13.700
One of the original Nick Jr. cartoons, The
World of David the Gnome was a gentle show

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.660
about magical creatures and forest animals.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:21.140
This richly animated series followed around
tiny, impressively bearded David and his gnome

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.980
family and friends as he went about his quiet
gnome life.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.210
According to lore, gnomes live to the age
of 400, and for the run of the show, David

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.300
and his wife Lisa were right around 399.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.180
And so, on the series finale, death came for
David, Lisa, and another gnome named Casper.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.080
Or, rather, they came for death.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:44.920
Doing what 400-years-old gnomes do, they ritualistically
accepted their fate, walking to the aptly-named

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.860
Mountains of Beyond, where they transformed
into trees.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:57.860
Mighty Max

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.639
A pocket-size toy line of action figures and
playsets called Mighty Max reached stores

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:03.160
in 1992.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:09.220
A year later, the Mighty Max TV series hit
the air, adding a surprisingly rich backstory

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.940
and mythology to those cheap pieces of plastic.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:16.140
It begins with Max receiving a statue that
contains an ancient, teleportation-enabling

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.820
baseball cap that also leads him to do battle
with a hideous monster called the Skullmaster.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:25.440
Mighty Max was one of the most violent kids'
shows of the era, as the Skullmaster routinely

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.580
and graphically killed people.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:31.750
In the final episode, he even kills Max's
friends, a prophetic fowl named Virgil and

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:32.600
a viking named Norman.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:37.640
So Max uses his baseball cap to travel back
in time to the events of the first episode.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:42.640
He then goes forth to relive the entire series,
only with an eye this time toward an outcome

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.040
where his best friends don't die.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:46.560
"Wonder who could have sent it.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:48.320
Wait a second.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.320
This is deja vu all over again!"

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:53.480
ReBoot

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:59.440
Imagine, if you will, a time in which children
were not computer-savvy, and we needed semi-educational

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.040
TV shows to familiarize them with the basic
technology.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:08.070
That time was the 1990s, a period in which
ABC's Saturday morning lineup included a show

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:08.900
called ReBoot.

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:14.040
Set inside a computer city called Mainframe,
the action involved a "guardian" named Bob

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.280
who protected the city against viruses named
Hexadecimal and Megabyte.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.750
ReBoot could be a little corny at times, but
it also demystified computing terms for millions

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:23.700
of tech-minded kids.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.260
Many of those same kids were likely traumatized
by the surprisingly dark ending.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:34.240
Megabyte, in the form of a Trojan horse virus,
gains access to the good guys' lair and sets

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.060
out to infect every program in existence.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:40.160
In other words, he destroys the world of the
show and the heroes don't know how to stop

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:40.680
it.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:45.000
"Prepare yourselves for the hunt."

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:47.800
Digimon Tamers

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:53.570
Part of the Digimon universe of cartoons and
games, Digimon Tamers was a one-season wonder

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:57.560
about cute animals that are also magical fighting
monsters trained by children.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:02.400
On the final episode, the various Digimon
and their human companions wage war against

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.220
the evil D-Reaper.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.660
They prevail, sending the bad guy back into
the digital world from whence he came.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:12.720
But it only works because the Digimon themselves
have to get sucked into oblivion as well.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.020
One by one, the cute little guys disappear
as their heartbroken trainers look on.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:22.280
Sure, the cliffhanger seems to suggest there's
some sort of portal to that other realm, but

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:26.400
for all intents and purposes, those Digimon
are gone, gone, gone.

